1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,100
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:08,941
This is chapter thirty-eight: stream gatherers, final in Java 24.

3
00:00:09,391 --> 00:00:16,463
Since Java 8, you could extend the end of a stream with a custom collector, but the middle was closed.

4
00:00:16,463 --> 00:00:18,959
Filter, map, flatMap, and that's it.

5
00:00:18,959 --> 00:00:24,853
So batching, running totals and deduplicating by key all meant abandoning the stream.

6
00:00:24,853 --> 00:00:26,586
Gatherers close that gap.

7
00:00:27,486 --> 00:00:31,704
Here's batching orders into groups of a hundred before Java 24.

8
00:00:31,704 --> 00:00:40,542
A loop, a current batch, and a check at the end for the trailing partial batch, which is the line most hand-written versions forget.

9
00:00:40,992 --> 00:00:49,359
Same story for a running total, deduplicating by SKU, and calling a vendor API per SKU at most twenty at a time.

10
00:00:49,359 --> 00:00:55,335
parallelStream can't do that last one, because it blocks the shared common pool.

11
00:00:55,335 --> 00:01:01,685
Each needed state carried between elements, and the stream API had nowhere to put it.

12
00:01:02,585 --> 00:01:07,738
A Gatherer is to intermediate operations what a Collector is to terminal ones.

13
00:01:07,738 --> 00:01:16,260
It has four matching parts: an initializer for state, an integrator per element, a combiner for parallel streams, and a finisher.

14
00:01:16,260 --> 00:01:23,791
The difference is that a gatherer pushes elements downstream as it goes, instead of accumulating into a container.

15
00:01:24,241 --> 00:01:26,526
Control flows through two booleans.

16
00:01:26,526 --> 00:01:32,730
Pushing downstream returns false when nothing downstream wants more, say a limit was satisfied.

17
00:01:32,730 --> 00:01:37,039
And the integrator returns false to say this gatherer is finished.

18
00:01:37,039 --> 00:01:42,459
That's what keeps laziness and short-circuiting working through a custom operation.

19
00:01:43,359 --> 00:01:45,051
There are five built-ins.

20
00:01:45,051 --> 00:01:50,463
windowFixed groups elements into lists of n, including the trailing partial one.

21
00:01:50,913 --> 00:01:56,268
windowSliding produces overlapping windows that advance one element at a time.

22
00:01:56,718 --> 00:02:00,281
fold accumulates everything into one value at the end.

23
00:02:00,731 --> 00:02:04,656
scan emits the running accumulation, one value per input.

24
00:02:05,106 --> 00:02:12,334
And mapConcurrent applies a function on virtual threads, at most limit at a time, preserving order.

25
00:02:12,334 --> 00:02:16,861
That's the one that changes how you write I/O-bound pipelines.

26
00:02:17,761 --> 00:02:20,694
Batching becomes one line in the pipeline.

27
00:02:20,694 --> 00:02:24,256
windowFixed of a hundred, then dispatch each batch.

28
00:02:24,256 --> 00:02:29,494
The trailing partial batch is emitted by its finisher, so that bug is gone.

29
00:02:29,944 --> 00:02:34,653
A running total is scan, starting from zero and adding each line.

30
00:02:34,653 --> 00:02:37,624
It emits the total so far for every line.

31
00:02:38,074 --> 00:02:44,162
And a five-minute moving average is windowSliding of five, then averaging each window.

32
00:02:44,162 --> 00:02:49,189
Before gatherers, that needed an indexed loop over a materialised list.

33
00:02:50,089 --> 00:02:51,883
Here's the important one.

34
00:02:51,883 --> 00:02:57,410
For each SKU, call the warehouse, a two-hundred-millisecond blocking request.

35
00:02:57,410 --> 00:03:06,814
mapConcurrent of twenty runs at most twenty at once, on virtual threads, and keeps the results in order, inside an ordinary stream.

36
00:03:07,264 --> 00:03:13,345
Compare parallelStream, which runs on the shared common pool, sized for CPU work.

37
00:03:13,345 --> 00:03:21,454
Blocking I/O there starves every other parallel stream in the JVM, and you can't bound concurrency per call.

38
00:03:21,454 --> 00:03:27,835
mapConcurrent gives you a bound, order preservation, and cancellation when one fails.

39
00:03:27,835 --> 00:03:32,565
For I/O fan-out in a stream on Java 24, it's the right default.

40
00:03:33,465 --> 00:03:35,326
Writing your own is short.

41
00:03:35,326 --> 00:03:39,118
Here's distinctBy, the most-wanted missing operation.

42
00:03:39,118 --> 00:03:42,480
The initializer creates a HashSet of seen keys.

43
00:03:42,480 --> 00:03:48,633
The integrator adds each element's key, and pushes the element only if the key is new.

44
00:03:49,083 --> 00:03:50,448
Two details matter.

45
00:03:50,448 --> 00:03:57,413
It uses ofSequential, because a mutable set has no sensible combiner, so it's never parallelised.

46
00:03:57,413 --> 00:03:59,783
That's correct, not a limitation.

47
00:03:59,783 --> 00:04:08,113
And it returns the result of push, not true, so if a limit of ten downstream is satisfied, the whole pipeline stops.

48
00:04:09,013 --> 00:04:10,308
Production rules.

49
00:04:10,308 --> 00:04:12,594
Reach for the built-ins first.

50
00:04:12,594 --> 00:04:16,405
Use mapConcurrent for I/O fan-out inside a stream.

51
00:04:16,405 --> 00:04:19,072
Use ofSequential for mutable state.

52
00:04:19,072 --> 00:04:21,053
Return the result of push.

53
00:04:21,053 --> 00:04:25,397
And package reusable gatherers as static factory methods.

54
00:04:25,847 --> 00:04:28,947
Never use parallelStream for blocking I/O.

55
00:04:28,947 --> 00:04:32,787
Don't write a gatherer where map or filter would do.

56
00:04:32,787 --> 00:04:37,142
Keep state inside the initializer, never shared outside it.

57
00:04:37,142 --> 00:04:39,948
And never ignore push returning false.

58
00:04:40,848 --> 00:04:41,508
To recap.

59
00:04:41,508 --> 00:04:48,696
A gatherer extends the middle of a stream, with an initializer, integrator, combiner and finisher.

60
00:04:48,696 --> 00:04:52,070
The built-ins handle windows, scans and folds.

61
00:04:52,070 --> 00:04:58,597
And mapConcurrent gives bounded, ordered I/O on virtual threads, right inside a pipeline.

62
00:04:59,047 --> 00:05:05,213
Next is chapter thirty-nine: the foreign function and memory API, and the Vector API.

63
00:05:05,213 --> 00:05:06,663
Thanks for watching.
